Looking at the production code change in `AbstractReflectionRoute.java`:

**Before:**
```java
private static Object invoke(Method method, Object target, Object[] arguments) throws Throwable {
    method.setAccessible(true);

    try {
      return method.invoke(target, arguments);
    } catch (InvocationTargetException e) {
      throw e.getCause();
    }
```

**After:**
```java
private static Object invoke(Method method, Object target, Object[] arguments) throws Throwable {
    try {
      method.setAccessible(true);
      return method.invoke(target, arguments);
    } catch (InvocationTargetException e) {
      throw e.getCause();
    }
```

The change moves `method.setAccessible(true)` inside the `try` block. The `setAccessible` call can throw `SecurityException` (an unchecked exception), and this doesn't change behavior in that regard. The functional behavior is identical — `setAccessible(true)` is still called before `method.invoke(...)`. The only difference is that if `setAccessible` throws a `SecurityException`, it's now inside the try-catch block, but the catch only handles `InvocationTargetException`, so a `SecurityException` would still propagate normally.

This change is essentially a refactor (moving a line inside a try block) with no observable behavioral difference in normal or edge cases. The rest of the diff is new test files and a class rename (`ErrorPagesTest` → `ErrorPageTest`).

The production code change is benign — no logic errors, no broken functionality, no regression.

