    if (tail.isFirstBlock()) { break; }
    tail = tail.pop();
}
mTailBlock = tail;
```

New code:
```java
do {
    tail.unlockToSavepoint(this, 0);
    tail = tail.pop();
} while (tail != null);
mTailBlock = null;
```

The comment added to `unlockToSavepoint` says: *"If target size is zero, caller MUST pop and discard the block. Otherwise, the block size will be zero, which is illegal."*

In the new `do...while` loop, `tail.unlockToSavepoint(this, 0)` is called (target size 0), then `tail = tail.pop()`. The `pop()` method sets `mPrev = null` and returns the previous block. This seems correct - after unlocking to size 0, we pop it. The loop continues until `tail` is null. Setting `mTailBlock = null` is correct.

### Second changed hunk (parent is Lock case):

Old code:
```java
while (true) {
    if (tail.isFirstBlock()) {
        tail.unlockToSavepoint(this, 1);
        break;
    }
    tail.unlockToSavepoint(this, 0);
    tail = tail.pop();
}
mTailBlock = tail;
```

New code:
```java
while (true) {
    Block prev = tail.peek();
    if (prev == null) {
        tail.unlockToSavepoint(this, 1);
        break;
    }
    tail.unlockToSavepoint(this, 0);
    tail.discard();
    tail = prev;
}
mTailBlock = tail;
```

Here `peek()` returns `mPrev` without modifying. When `prev == null`, we unlock to size 1 (keeping one lock - the parent's lock). When `prev != null`, we unlock to size 0, then `discard()` sets `mPrev = null`, then move to `prev`.

The new `discard()` method just sets `mPrev = null`. The old `pop()` also set `mPrev = null` and returned prev. The difference: old code used `pop()` which returns prev AND sets mPrev=null. New code uses `tail.discard()` + `tail = prev` (where prev was already captured by `peek()`). This is semantically equivalent.

### Removed `isFirstBlock()`:
Replaced by checking `peek() == null`. Equivalent logic.

